WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.205 --> 00:00:02.055
A robot watches a demonstration.

2
00:00:02.065 --> 00:00:03.375
Has it learned the task,

3
00:00:03.385 --> 00:00:05.152
or just copied the motion?

4
00:00:05.698 --> 00:00:07.908
That's the question
behind our survey,

5
00:00:07.918 --> 00:00:10.428
In-Context Learning for Robots:

6
00:00:10.438 --> 00:00:12.468
Methods and Applications.

7
00:00:12.478 --> 00:00:14.508
We map how demonstrations,

8
00:00:14.518 --> 00:00:16.248
corrections, and experience

9
00:00:16.258 --> 00:00:18.048
shape behavior without updating

10
00:00:18.058 --> 00:00:20.209
the model's weights
during deployment.

11
00:00:20.705 --> 00:00:22.676
So where does that context go?

12
00:00:23.172 --> 00:00:25.442
Into four kinds of execution:

13
00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:26.822
predicting actions,

14
00:00:26.832 --> 00:00:28.982
transferring geometric motion,

15
00:00:28.992 --> 00:00:30.602
planning with world models,

16
00:00:30.612 --> 00:00:33.159
or composing skills and programs.

17
00:00:33.672 --> 00:00:35.427
Why does that distinction matter?

18
00:00:35.998 --> 00:00:37.128
Because the same video

19
00:00:37.138 --> 00:00:39.108
can guide different mechanisms.

20
00:00:39.118 --> 00:00:41.628
Each one has different
requirements for transfer,

21
00:00:41.638 --> 00:00:43.285
and different ways to fail.

22
00:00:43.832 --> 00:00:46.319
If success goes up,
isn't that enough?

23
00:00:46.865 --> 00:00:48.295
Try two tests.

24
00:00:48.305 --> 00:00:49.435
Keep the scene fixed

25
00:00:49.445 --> 00:00:51.295
and change the demonstration.

26
00:00:51.305 --> 00:00:52.855
Then keep the task fixed

27
00:00:52.865 --> 00:00:54.355
and change the object.

28
00:00:54.365 --> 00:00:56.095
Does behavior follow the teaching,

29
00:00:56.105 --> 00:00:57.584
and does it transfer?

30
00:00:58.192 --> 00:01:00.835
For example, picking up a mug
by its handle?

31
00:01:01.458 --> 00:01:03.368
Exactly. Reaching the goal

32
00:01:03.378 --> 00:01:05.108
is only part of the story.

33
00:01:05.118 --> 00:01:06.728
The robot should also preserve

34
00:01:06.738 --> 00:01:09.477
the demonstrated contact
and action order.

35
00:01:09.972 --> 00:01:12.135
And what should
researchers take away?

36
00:01:12.638 --> 00:01:14.788
A framework for comparing methods,

37
00:01:14.798 --> 00:01:16.888
designing stronger evaluations,

38
00:01:16.898 --> 00:01:18.568
and asking whether experience

39
00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:20.308
helps with the next task.

40
00:01:20.318 --> 00:01:21.568
Explore the full survey

41
00:01:21.578 --> 00:01:23.128
and our continuously updated

42
00:01:23.138 --> 00:01:24.401
literature collection.